# Clean Code — Objects and Data Structures

Objects and data structures serve different purposes. An **object** hides its data behind abstractions and exposes behaviour. A **data structure** exposes its data and has no meaningful behaviour. Choose one; mixing them creates hybrids that are harder to extend in either direction.

## OBJ-01 — Hide internal structure

An object should not expose its fields or internal representation. Callers work through its methods, not its internals.

```
// bad — exposes raw coordinates
point.x = 10
point.y = 20

// good — exposes intent
point.setCartesian(10, 20)
```

## OBJ-02 — Avoid hybrid structures

Do not create classes that are half-object (with business logic) and half-data structure (with public fields). Hybrids make it hard to add new functions and hard to add new data types.

```
// bad — public fields exposed AND business logic mixed in
class Employee
  public name
  public salary
  public department

  function calculateBonus()
    if department == "sales"
      return salary * 0.2
    end if
    return salary * 0.1
  end function
end class

// good — separate concerns
struct EmployeeData   // pure data structure
  name
  salary
  department
end struct

class BonusCalculator   // pure object
  function calculate(employeeData)
    if employeeData.department == "sales"
      return employeeData.salary * 0.2
    end if
    return employeeData.salary * 0.1
  end function
end class
```

## OBJ-03 — Keep classes small

A class should have a small, focused set of responsibilities. High cohesion — most methods use most of the fields — is the signal that a class is the right size.

## OBJ-04 — Do one thing

Apply the Single Responsibility Principle: a class should have one reason to change. If you can describe two independent changes that would each require editing the class, split it.

## OBJ-05 — Prefer few instance variables

Cohesion is high when each method uses most of the class's fields. A large number of fields used by only a few methods signals that the class should be split.

```
// bad — low cohesion: reportTitle/reportDate only used by two methods,
//       connectionString/timeout only used by two others
class DataManager
  reportTitle
  reportDate
  connectionString
  timeout
  cacheSize
  retryCount

  function generateReport() ... end function
  function formatReport() ... end function
  function openConnection() ... end function
  function executeQuery() ... end function
end class

// good — split into two cohesive classes
class ReportBuilder
  title
  date
  function generate() ... end function
  function format() ... end function
end class

class DatabaseClient
  connectionString
  timeout
  cacheSize
  retryCount
  function open() ... end function
  function execute() ... end function
end class
```

## OBJ-06 — Base classes must not know about their derivatives

A base class should compile and function without any knowledge of the classes that extend it. Dependencies flow upward toward abstraction, never downward toward implementation.

```
// bad — base class checks for a specific subtype
class Shape
  function draw()
    if typeOf(this) == Circle
      drawCircle()
    else if typeOf(this) == Square
      drawSquare()
    end if
  end function
end class

// good — base class defines the contract; subtypes fulfil it
class Shape
  function draw()   // abstract
end class

class Circle extends Shape
  function draw()
    // circle-specific drawing
  end function
end class
```

## OBJ-07 — Prefer many small functions over passing behaviour as a flag

Rather than passing a block of logic or a mode selector into a function to change its behaviour, define separate named functions for each behaviour. This is consistent with FN-06 and PRCP-05.

## OBJ-08 — Prefer non-static methods

Static methods cannot be overridden, which makes polymorphism and testing harder. Prefer instance methods and inject the object where needed.
